00:00:02.629 --> 00:00:04.630 align:start position:0%
i'm carlos garcianson i'm a research  professor at the national university of  mexico  i've been working trying to understand  and control complex systems for more  than 20 years  and we use basically computer  simulations to do this  when you study a complex system like  urban traffic  or crowds you have hundreds or thousands  of components  and even if you have the equations to  describe them you basically need a  computer to solve them  to see how different types of  interactions will lead to different  global behaviors  we have lots of examples of  self-organizing systems in nature like  flocks of birds schools of fish herds of  cattle  crowds and we have  a certain understanding of how these  behave  in different conditions and this  understanding  can be useful when we study urban  mobility  be it with passengers in public  transportation systems  or with drivers in highways or in  streets that  depending on the individual behavior of  each person  the global pattern might be different  we're interested in  how can you regulate the global behavior  by adjusting  the interactions between drivers or  between  passengers in public transport  a characteristic feature of complex  systems is that the relevant  interactions between their components  and these interactions generate novel  information that is not present in  initial nor boundary conditions  and this limits predictability thus we  need  adaptability in order to complement this  lack of predictability and if we have  both  then we will be able to face complexity  much better than if we have  just one  we have proposed self financing  controllers for traffic lights and these  basically are independent intersections  that have sensors and they give  preference to the streets with the  highest demand and even when there's no  communication between the intersections  they can self-organize into so-called  green waves  because they basically form platoons and  these platoons when they are of a  certain size  they will be able to trigger green  lights even before they reach the  intersection so  cars don't have to stop unless there's  another plateau crossing the  intersection  and this adaptive mechanism  does not depend on the hour of the day  because nothing has to be programmed the  system  adapts itself and if for example there's  a  soccer match and then there's lots of  people coming out from a stadium  then the controllers will adapt without  human intervention  and of course this is much more  efficient than trying to predict the  traffic flow that we know  is not predictable  if you don't have a proper theory then  it's difficult to find solutions  but if you don't have solutions or if  you don't apply the theory  in the real world then it's difficult to  distinguish between competing theories  so uh science is pragmatic and we end up  using the theories that are more useful  in the real world so  in my work we try to combine both and we  have  theories that are based on successful  applications but also  propose solutions to real-world problems  based on theoretical advances
 

